# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Scratch output from the film harness. The shipped films themselves
# (server/static/films/*.webm) ARE committed — an attendee unzips this
# repo and plays, with no build step and no ffmpeg — but the generator
# (backstage/films/) is what you edit. See
# docs/TUTORIAL_PLAN.md §4.1.
reports/films/
reports/filmsheets/

# Test / tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Editor / OS
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# Local agent/tooling settings
.claude/

# Secrets / local config (never commit)
*.env
.env*
connections.toml
secrets.toml
*.pem
*.key

# Local file-backed store output (SOC_BACKEND=file) + sweep artifacts.
# Generated CSVs are regenerable; the summary .md reports are docs (kept).
/seasons/
/seasons_battery/
/seasons_battery.bak_*/
/_watch_seasons/
/_perf_seasons/
/_tmp_seasons_dbg/
/reports/*.csv
/reports/*.log
/reports/baseline_*.md
# Screenshots shot by the scratch harnesses (backstage/probes/_fx_*.py). Regenerable by
# re-running the harness, and several MB a session. The frozen/*.json fixtures
# under reports/ are deliberately tracked and unaffected by this.
/reports/**/*.png

# Battle-room bakes (`soc suite --record`). Output, not source: a bake is
# one person's suite run, they accumulate, and the room itself is copied
# in from sea_of_colours/evals/battles/room/. Committing these would put
# every attendee's runs in everyone else's diff.
/reports/battles/
/reports/battle-room-probe/

# Headless season records (`soc season`) and the local session stores
# they write through. Same argument as the bakes: one person's runs,
# regenerable, and a season's cards are megabytes of prompt text.
/reports/seasons/
/.soc_sessions/
/.soc_probe*/

# Cached single-turn runs from the fast diff loop. Keyed on a hash of
# the agent's own source, so they are worthless to anyone else and
# regenerate in one model call.
/.soc_livecache/
/reports/cards-probe/

# Turn Lab — its own local store. The clones are scratch; the boards
# are not, and they ship (v1.42).
#
# The boards used to be excluded on the grounds that they regenerate
# from `python -m turnlab.mint`. Two things were wrong with that. Only
# the minted half regenerates — a board grabbed out of a played season
# needs that season, which for the two carrying real agent journals
# means somebody's Snowflake account, so a fresh clone could never
# rebuild them at all. And a frozen turn is only worth anything if it
# is the *same* turn for everyone: the recorded V12 baselines are diffed
# against, so a locally-regenerated board would quietly compare a fork
# to a baseline from a different night.
#
# They fit because `python -m turnlab.pack` trims each board to the one
# night it can actually read and gzips it — 107MB of replay frames down
# to 1.1MB. Pack before committing a new board.
#
# Boards only, hence the whitelist. The directory also accumulates the
# throwaway `LABRUN_` clones and the full source seasons that minting
# played out to cut the boards from — one of those is 24MB of frames on
# its own, and nothing reads it once the boards exist.
turnlab/data/*
!turnlab/data/LAB_*/

# Forks handed between teammates by `soc share`. Never committed: the
# published copy of an agent is its directory, and a parcel is a
# snapshot in flight. Ignored rather than merely discouraged because
# `soc push` refuses to publish while anything outside your own folder
# has changed — so a parcel left in the repo root would block the
# push it has nothing to do with.
*.socfork

# Film voiceover intermediates (backstage/films/voice/). Same argument as
# the films themselves: the narration that ships is muxed into the
# committed .webm, so the per-line .wav clips are build scratch.
#
# The voice models are excluded for a different reason — they are ~60MB
# of ONNX weights fetched from upstream, identical for everyone, and
# re-downloadable in one command. What IS tracked is the scripts/
# directory of narration text, because that is written by a human and is
# the only part nothing can regenerate.
backstage/films/voice/models/
backstage/films/voice/clips/
backstage/films/voice/build/

# The station beats' own season (backstage/films/.station-season/). A real
# played season is ~200MB of replay frames, and it is reproducible: the
# seed lives in make_trailer.py and `--stations` replays it if the
# directory is missing. Keep the clips, not the store that made them.
backstage/films/.station-season/

# Forked agents (v1.56). Main carries the BASELINE only. A fork is one
# team's work and lives in their own fork of this repo — that is the
# whole point of `soc push`, and an agent landing here would hand
# everyone pulling from upstream a conflict in someone else's code.
#
# Ignored rather than merely discouraged because `soc collect` and a
# day's play leave a working tree full of other people's harnesses, and
# a single `git add -A` then stages all of them. That has happened: 31
# fork directories, 1,603 files, sitting in the index.
#
# Shipped harnesses are re-included by name, so adding one is a
# deliberate edit here rather than something that drifts in.
sea_of_colours/orchestrator_2/harnesses/*/
!sea_of_colours/orchestrator_2/harnesses/tabula_v12/
!sea_of_colours/orchestrator_2/harnesses/emp_harvest_test/

# A league's own results (`soc cup`). One organiser's run of one day:
# regenerable, and the bracket that matters gets published, not merged.
/reports/league/

# Trailer byproduct (v1.56). The trailer is not a repo artifact — the
# finished cut gets uploaded, and everything that made it is scratch.
# `server/static/media/` alone is 333MB of renders, and the scratch
# tree beside it has reached 3.7GB.
#
# Note if you are wiring the landing page: `server/static/landing.html`
# is the one thing that reads from media/, so a fresh clone has no hero
# loop until someone re-shoots or drops the files in.
/server/static/media/
/weaponx/
/.praxis-work/
